# CODEOWNERS is the human-visibility half of the spine policy: it puts a named person on the review
# of anything touching the guarantees the platform exists to provide. The ENFORCING half is
# `.github/scripts/pr-gates.mjs`, which fails as a required status check — CODEOWNERS only requests a
# review, and only blocks if you also turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in branch protection.
#
# Keep both. The check is what stops an agent; the ownership is what tells you it happened.

* @abrahamFerga

# The spine — RBAC before the model, approval-first writes, tenant isolation, audit, secrets.
# Casewell is a thin host over the Cortex platform packages, so its spine is three concrete files
# rather than the layered directories a self-hosted product would have:
#   LegalDbContext    — per-entity HasQueryFilter, i.e. tenant isolation
#   LegalModule       — the manifest: permissions, role baselines
#   LegalToolSource   — tool declarations, including which writes require approval
/src/Casewell.Legal/Persistence/     @abrahamFerga
/src/Casewell.Legal/LegalModule.cs   @abrahamFerga
/src/Casewell.Legal/LegalToolSource.cs @abrahamFerga

# Kept for when the host grows its own layers — these match nothing today, and should start
# matching the day someone adds them rather than the day someone remembers to update this file.
/src/**/Authorization/     @abrahamFerga
/src/**/Approvals/         @abrahamFerga

# The harness itself. An agent that can edit its own gates has no gates.
/.github/                  @abrahamFerga
/CODEOWNERS                @abrahamFerga
/nuget.config              @abrahamFerga
/workflow.json             @abrahamFerga
